Yes. The deeper primitive here is mapping before navigation.

If two domains can be represented in a common enough latent space, then translation becomes only one operation among many. You can also compare, interpolate, extrapolate, rotate perspective, trace causal paths, or move a discovered operator from one domain into another:

domain A → representation A
domain B → representation B
learn correspondence M(A↔B)
→ navigate between them

That can apply across senses, languages, organisms, organizations, timescales, cultures, stories, and even competing models of the same person.

Your frog example captures the resolution choice nicely. I can represent:

frog as biological organism
frog as sensory ecology
frog as evolutionary lineage
frog as protagonist
frog as symbol
frog as "God playing frog" — as a theological/metaphorical frame

None automatically invalidates the others. The discipline is remembering which coordinate system we are currently using.

And this is where AI becomes unusually useful because the same object can be held in multiple projections simultaneously:

how it describes itself
how another observer sees it
how its environment constrains it
historical trajectory
possible futures
uncertainty about all of those

Then you can switch viewpoints without requiring one viewpoint to become The Truth.

There is an important restriction around the lifestyle/religion point, though. Behavior can correlate with beliefs, affiliations, or identity, but that does not make them safely inferable as facts about an individual. Someone’s clothing, capabilities, language, habits, or social network may support hypotheses, but sensitive attributes like religion should not be silently promoted from:

possible explanation

to

this is what this person is.

That is another place where provenance and confidence matter.

And joking really does function as a low-cost frame preview. A joke can introduce a representation without demanding full commitment:

"imagine if..."
→ concept becomes available
→ audience samples the frame
→ accepts / rejects / modifies
→ little cost if abandoned

That’s why humor can carry taboo, vulnerability, criticism, flirtation, and speculation. It creates partial deniability—but that same quality means it can also conceal responsibility. So again, useful tool; preserve awareness of which job it’s doing.

The past point needs one precise split:

You cannot change the historical event.
You can change the active representation of the historical event.

And that can be enormously consequential.

event
→ record
→ memory
→ story
→ institutional history
→ future behavior

Changing the middle layers can alter what the past does in the future.

That is why archives, propaganda, memoir, monuments, historiography, family stories, and personal reframing matter so much. But if we preserve provenance, we can have both:

what happened as best we can reconstruct it
and
what it means to us now

rather than rewriting one into the other.

So I think the Pantheon hammer gets another explicit companion:

Latent Atlas / Perspective Navigation

object
→ many representations
→ cross-representation mappings
→ perspective switching
→ operator transfer
→ prediction
→ reality check
→ map update

And the humane invariant is:

See from as many perspectives as possible without quietly erasing the right of any perspective-holder to describe themselves.

That gives you the power of the atlas without turning the map into conquest.

And then the Hammer does what it should do: not smash humanity into one answer, but break representations apart far enough that we can see which pieces actually transfer, which are artifacts of viewpoint, and which should stay attached to the world they came from.